GNU QUIC
========

GNU QUIC is a QUIC (RFC 9000), TLS 1.2/1.3 and DTLS 1.2/1.3 engine
written in C for the GNU system.  It is the substrate on which HTTP/3
and DNS over QUIC (DoQ) clients and servers are meant to be built.

It is a sibling of the Gumdrop (Java) and Hopf (Rust) frameworks and
follows the same design: a thin cryptographic layer, incremental push
parsers that deliver semantic events, and reactive state machines that
never block and never own a socket.

Status
------

Pre-alpha.  What exists today:

  crypto   libgcrypt-backed hashes, HMAC, HKDF, AES-GCM and
           ChaCha20-Poly1305, header protection masks
  frame    QUIC frame push parser and writer (all RFC 9000 frames plus
           RFC 9221 DATAGRAM)
  varint   RFC 9000 variable-length integers
  packet   packet numbers, long/short header codecs, Version
           Negotiation, Retry, stateless reset (QUIC v1 and v2)
  protect  Initial and traffic key derivation, key update, in-place
           packet protection, Retry integrity tag (checked against the
           RFC 9001 and RFC 9369 appendix A vectors)
  tparams  transport parameter codec with RFC 9000 validation
  kx       key exchange: X25519, secp256r1, secp384r1 and the RFC 10024
           ML-KEM hybrids, over libgcrypt
  tlsmsg   TLS 1.3 handshake message codecs and writer (RFC 8446 s4)
  keysched TLS 1.3 key schedule and transcript hash, SHA-256/SHA-384
           (reproduces the RFC 8448 trace)
  tls      TLS 1.3 client and server handshake engines (QUIC and TCP modes)
  record   TLS 1.3 record layer (RFC 8448 records reproduced exactly)
  tlsconn  TLS 1.3 over a byte stream: engine plus record layer;
           client and server, interoperating with OpenSSL and GnuTLS
  tickets  stateless session ticket key ring; PSK resumption in the
           handshake engines (RFC 8448 section 4 reproduced)
  sign     private key loading and CertificateVerify signatures
           (GnuTLS backend; ML-DSA needs a backend that has it)
  trust    trust anchors and certificate chain validation (GnuTLS
           backend behind a replaceable interface)
  policy   the closed set of versions, suites, groups and signature
           schemes the library will ever negotiate
  tls12ks  TLS 1.2 PRF, extended master secret, key block, Finished
  tls12msg TLS 1.2 handshake message codecs
  record12 TLS 1.2 record layer (AES-GCM and ChaCha20-Poly1305)
  tls12    TLS 1.2 client and server handshake engines: ECDHE on
           secp256r1 with AEAD suites, mandatory extended master secret
           and secure renegotiation indication, RFC 5077 tickets,
           mutual authentication
  tls12conn  TLS 1.2 over a byte stream; interoperating with OpenSSL and
           GnuTLS
  dtlsrec  DTLS 1.3 records: unified header, record number encryption,
           anti-replay
  dtlshs   DTLS 1.3 handshake fragments, reassembly and ACK codecs
  dtls     DTLS 1.3 association: the TLS 1.3 engine in DTLS mode with
           flights, ACKs, retransmission timers and KeyUpdate
  dtlscookie  stateless cookie exchange for servers; interoperating with
           wolfSSL
  dtls12rec  DTLS 1.2 records (AES-GCM and ChaCha20-Poly1305)
  dtls12   DTLS 1.2 association: the TLS 1.2 engine in DTLS mode with
           whole-flight retransmission
  dtls12cookie  stateless HelloVerifyRequest for servers; interoperating
           with OpenSSL, GnuTLS and wolfSSL

  ranges   sets of half-open ranges (received packet numbers, stream
           gaps, acknowledged and lost data)
  stream   stream send and receive state machines (RFC 9000 section 3):
           in-order push delivery, flow control, final size, reset
  token    address validation tokens: authenticated, address-bound, keyed
           by a rotating key (Retry and NEW_TOKEN)
  listen   stateless admission of a first Initial: Version Negotiation,
           Retry, token validation
  (conn also does Version Negotiation on the client and RFC 9368
           compatible negotiation between QUIC v1 and v2; v2 is preferred by
           default, and tickets are bound to the version)
  (conn also does path validation, peer migration and client-initiated
           migration over caller-supplied opaque addresses)
  endpoint many connections behind one socket: routing by connection ID,
           admission (Version Negotiation, Retry), stateless reset, a
           deadline heap; DATAGRAM frames (RFC 9221) in conn
  conn     QUIC connection: handshake over CRYPTO frames, packet number
           spaces, ACKs, RTT, loss detection and probe timeouts,
           streams, flow control, connection IDs, key update, idle
           timeout, close and stateless reset; NewReno congestion control
           (RFC 9002 section 7); interoperating with quiche

Not yet written: pacing and ECN, the server's
preferred_address, and the HTTP/3 and DoQ examples.  See the manual
(doc/gnuquic.texi) for the design, security policy and roadmap.

Building
--------

Requires libgcrypt >= 1.11 and, by default, GnuTLS >= 3.8.

  ./autogen.sh        # only when building from a repository checkout
  ./configure
  make
  make check

Use --without-gnutls to build without certificate validation.  The build
is expected to be free of compiler warnings.

